NSFileManager could not delete a file. Any file, for any application.

-[NSFileManager removeItemAtPath:error:] hands the job to NSFilesystemItemRemoveOperation, which does
not call unlink itself: it WALKS the path with nftw() and calls remove() from the callback. And
nftw() refuses any root that is not a directory. That is Apple's own libc source and not something
this port did: under __DARWIN_UNIX03 it stats the path and, when an nftw-style callback is supplied,
returns -1 with ENOTDIR for anything that is not a directory, before fts_open is ever called.

MEASURED, with a directory in the SAME RUN as the control, because "nftw is broken" and "nftw does
not like plain files" are different findings and only the second explains why a directory-based
instrument would have looked healthy:

    nftw(a plain file) rc=-1 errno=20 (Not a directory), callback fired 0 time(s)
    nftw(a directory)  rc=0,                             callback fired 4 time(s)

HOW IT PRESENTED, and the application was blamed for it. MoneyMoney stopped on "Could not delete the
temporary database file ... Please check the file permissions of the database directory", while the
directory was writable and rm removed that exact file from a container shell. A syscall level trace
settled it: the application NEVER ATTEMPTED the delete. The only unlinks in a whole run were our own
Wayland shm files. The application's wording was a GUESS about the cause, not a diagnosis.

The walk stays for directories, where it is the right tool and demonstrably works. A single non
directory is removed directly, through the same callback function, so the delegate hooks
shouldShouldRemoveItemAtPath and shouldProceedAfterError behave identically for one file. Real
Foundation does not remove files by walking them; this implementation carries a TODO next to it
saying as much.

AFTER THIS, MoneyMoney deletes its temporary database, creates a real one (307200 bytes and a
journal, where the file had been zero bytes), and OPENS ITS MAIN WINDOW: toolbar, sidebar, search
field and status bar. It then raises a different error, an unrecognized selector
-[NSUserNotificationCenter removeAllDeliveredNotifications], which is the next piece of work.
